From 235f75f6a454bd41c286d0890dad182fa357d1dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:38:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: Cap the maximum frequency on Snapdragon X

On the Qualcomm Snapdragon X series the CPUs have no energy-performance
preference and boost is off by default, so the boost switch can't tell
the profiles apart and the daemon falls back to the placeholder driver.
Windows on these machines implements its power modes in the OS, chiefly
by capping the processor's maximum performance per mode.

Give the cpufreq driver a second method, picked per scaling driver:
apple-cpufreq keeps the boost switch, and scmi and qcom-cpufreq-hw cap
each policy's scaling_max_freq at a share of the policy's highest
non-boost frequency, rounded to the nearest supported frequency: 60% in
power-saver and 75% in balanced, on battery and on AC alike, so that
switching from performance to balanced always backs off. The caps are
based on scaling_available_frequencies, because cpuinfo_max_freq keeps
the boost frequency once boost has been on. Generic OPP tables
(cpufreq-dt), as on many single-board computers, are left out: the caps
are only measured on Snapdragon X, and balanced being the default
profile would slow those machines down.

The caps method leaves the boost switch and the governor alone.
Performance, which is uncapped, allows the boost frequencies, also where
a kernel only reveals them in cpuinfo_max_freq once boost is on, so
whether they are used stays up to the boost switch. On the Surface Pro
11 tested here the firmware doesn't grant the X1E's boost level at all,
so the daemon has no business switching it.

The profile is re-applied after resume, for both methods, in case
anything changed the boost switch or lifted the caps. The caps are
lifted to the hardware maximum when the daemon stops, since a cap left
behind would slow the machine down until reboot. A cluster whose CPUs
are all offline returns EBUSY and is skipped when probing and when
capping; it is picked up, like a policy that appears later, at the next
profile change or resume, since the daemon's sandbox (PrivateNetwork=yes)
keeps udev events from reaching it. A policy that disappears is dropped.
A cluster that is offline when the daemon stops keeps its cap, which is
logged. Other write failures are reported, and a profile change only
fails if nothing could be capped.

On a Snapdragon X Elite (X1E-80-100), a sustained all-core job drew
31.5 W uncapped, 19.6 W at the balanced cap and 15.1 W at the
power-saver cap: 39% and 50% less energy for the same work, taking 16%
and 37% longer. Idle power was unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
